Bluetooth: btmtk: do not reset the controller when firmware is absent

btmtk_usb_setup() retries the firmware download once when
btmtk_setup_firmware_79xx() fails, guarding the retry with
BTMTK_FIRMWARE_DL_RETRY in btmtk_data->flags. The guard never holds.

btmtk_data is allocated inside the hci_dev by hci_alloc_dev_priv() in
btusb_probe(), and btmtk_reset_sync() reaches btusb_mtk_reset(), which calls
usb_queue_reset_device(). btusb supplies neither pre_reset nor post_reset, so
usb_reset_device() takes its unbind path and the driver is unbound and rebound
across the reset. btusb_disconnect() frees the hci_dev holding the flag and
btusb_probe() allocates a fresh, zeroed one, so BTMTK_FIRMWARE_DL_RETRY reads
clear on the next attempt. "Retry once" becomes once per probe, which is once
per reset, which does not terminate.

With the firmware file missing the loop runs until the machine is powered off.
odouglsantos recorded 398 resets in one boot and 1335 in another on an MT6639
(0489:e13a), one per firmware failure, roughly one every 0.58 seconds. Several
hundred resets leave the controller unable to answer a control transfer at
all: it stops enumerating, and because the standby rail keeps it powered a
reboot does not clear it. See
https://github.com/jetm/mediatek-mt7927-dkms/issues/23.

A subsystem reset cannot make a missing file appear, so skip the reset when
the firmware failed to load with -ENOENT. Failures a reset can plausibly clear
keep the existing single retry.

This patch is carried here for two reasons. The bug is present in 6.18, 7.0,
7.1 and 7.2 alike, so users on kernels below 7.1 - where this package builds
btusb and btmtk by default - are exposed on an ordinary boot whenever the
Bluetooth blob is absent. And it gives the reporter a way to confirm the fix
before it goes to linux-bluetooth.

Upstream submission is pending a Tested-by; drop this patch once it lands in
the kernel base this package builds from.

--- a/btmtk.c
+++ b/btmtk.c
@@ -1394,8 +1394,14 @@ int btmtk_usb_setup(struct hci_dev *hdev)
 						btmtk_usb_hci_wmt_sync,
 						dev_id);
 		if (err < 0) {
-			/* retry once if setup firmware error */
-			if (!test_and_set_bit(BTMTK_FIRMWARE_DL_RETRY, &btmtk_data->flags))
+			/*
+			 * Retry once if setup firmware error, but never when
+			 * the firmware file is absent: the reset unbinds the
+			 * driver, so the retry guard is lost with the hci_dev
+			 * and the retry repeats without bound.
+			 */
+			if (err != -ENOENT &&
+			    !test_and_set_bit(BTMTK_FIRMWARE_DL_RETRY, &btmtk_data->flags))
 				btmtk_reset_sync(hdev);
 			bt_dev_err(hdev, "Failed to set up firmware (%d)", err);
 			return err;
